course: geography
lesson: 32
title: Venice, the Ottomans and the Mediterranean
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Venice is a city built on more than a hundred small islands in a shallow lagoon, where enemy ships ran aground and armies could not march in; its people lived by trade, not by farming.
explain: To sail safely to the spice markets of the East, Venice held a chain of harbours down the Adriatic and in Greece, like a row of petrol stations along a long motorway.
explain: In 1337, when Europa Universalis 5 begins, the Ottomans were only a small beylik, a little Turkish princedom, in north-west Anatolia; within about 120 years they took Constantinople and grew into a huge empire.
explain: The game makes this sea a board of fleets and trade routes, which is fair, but it hides the people at the oars: galleys on both sides were often rowed by captives and enslaved people.
explain: After 1500 the great trade slowly moved out to the oceans, and Venice stayed rich but no longer central, until Napoleon ended the old republic in 1797.
fact: Venice was built on islands in a shallow lagoon at the top of the Adriatic Sea.
fact: Venice was a merchant republic ruled by an elected Doge (a duke chosen for life) and councils of noble families.
fact: Venice held a chain of ports and islands along the Adriatic and in Greece, including Crete and later Cyprus.
fact: The Arsenal of Venice was a huge state shipyard that could build ships one after another, almost like a factory, and it was one of the largest workplaces in Europe before the industrial age.
fact: The Ottomans began as a small state in north-west Anatolia, in today's Turkey, around 1300 and grew into an empire across south-eastern Europe, the Middle East and North Africa.
fact: The Ottoman sultan Mehmed II captured Constantinople in 1453, ending the Byzantine Empire.
fact: Constantinople, later called Istanbul, became the Ottoman capital and controlled the straits between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean.
fact: The Ottoman Empire ruled many peoples and religions, including Turks, Greeks, Arabs, Armenians, Slavs and Jews, and many Jews expelled from Spain in 1492 were welcomed there.
fact: The Ottomans took Christian boys from Balkan villages through the devshirme, converted them and trained them as soldiers or officials, and the elite Janissary infantry was recruited this way.
fact: In 1571 a Christian fleet led by Spain, Venice and the Pope defeated the Ottoman navy at Lepanto off western Greece.
fact: The Ottomans rebuilt their fleet within about a year, and Venice still lost Cyprus, so Lepanto changed less than people thought at the time.
fact: Mediterranean galleys were often rowed by prisoners, captives and enslaved people, on both Christian and Muslim sides.
fact: After 1500 the Mediterranean slowly became less central to world trade as Atlantic and ocean routes grew.
fact: The Republic of Venice lasted about a thousand years until Napoleon ended it in 1797.
exercise: Where was Venice built? | On islands in a shallow lagoon | On a high hill far from the sea | In a deep valley in the Alps | On a wide plain beside a river
exercise: Why was a lagoon a safe place for a city? | Enemy ships ran aground and armies could not march in | The water was too hot for enemy soldiers to wade across in summer | Lagoons are always hidden by thick fog from the sea | Nobody else knew the lagoon existed
exercise: Who ruled Venice? | An elected Doge with councils of nobles | A king chosen every ten years by the Pope | The Ottoman sultan, from his palace in Istanbul | A queen who inherited the throne from her mother
exercise: Why did Venice need ports along the route to the East? | To keep its ships safe and supplied on long voyages | To grow wheat for the city | To stop its own people from sailing away and leaving the city empty | To stop the rivers from flooding
exercise: Which large Greek island did Venice hold for centuries? | Crete | Sicily | Malta | Corsica
exercise: What was the Arsenal of Venice? | A huge state shipyard | A big market for spices | The palace of the Doge | A prison for pirates
exercise: How was the Arsenal like a factory? | Ships were built one after another in organised steps | It was powered by huge steam engines that turned the saws | It made carriages and cannons as well as ships for sale | Robots and clever machines did most of the hard work there
exercise: Where did the Ottoman state begin? | In north-west Anatolia | In southern Spain | In the middle of Arabia | On the island of Crete
exercise: Who captured Constantinople in 1453? | Sultan Mehmed II | Napoleon | The Doge | Genghis Khan
exercise: Why did whoever held Constantinople control trade between two seas? | It sits on the straits between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean | It is the only city in the whole of Europe and Asia with a harbour | It had the biggest lighthouse in the world | All ships had to be built there
exercise: How did the Ottomans treat people of other religions? | They ruled many faiths, and many Jews expelled from Spain found refuge there | They let only Muslims live in the empire | They forced every person to speak Greek | They sent all Christians to the Americas
exercise: What was the devshirme? | Taking Christian boys to be trained as soldiers or officials | A tax that every village paid to the sultan in spices and silk | A type of Ottoman warship | A festival at the end of harvest
exercise: Which elite Ottoman soldiers were recruited through the devshirme? | The Janissaries | The Viking raiders | The samurai of Japan | The Knights Templar
exercise: Who won the sea battle of Lepanto in 1571? | A Christian fleet led by Spain, Venice and the Pope | The Ottoman navy, which sank almost every Christian ship | The English navy | The Portuguese navy
exercise: Who rowed many Mediterranean galleys? | Prisoners, captives and enslaved people on both sides | Only paid sailors who loved the sea | Only the captain's own family | Only volunteers from the Pope's army who rowed for their sins
exercise: Why did ocean routes hurt Venice after 1500? | Trade moved to the Atlantic and round Africa, away from the Mediterranean | Venice's lagoon froze over every winter | The Ottomans filled in the lagoon | Venice banned all ships from its harbour
exercise: Who ended the Republic of Venice in 1797? | Napoleon | Mehmed the Conqueror | Queen Victoria | Peter the Great
test: My mate says Lepanto smashed the Ottomans for good. Right? | No, they rebuilt their fleet in about a year and still took Cyprus | Yes, the Ottomans never had a fleet again after that day, and lost Cyprus too | Yes, and Venice took Constantinople back | No, the Ottomans won Lepanto
test: In EU5 the Ottomans start as a tiny beylik in 1337. What happened in real life? | They grew into an empire over three continents | They stayed a tiny state in Anatolia until about 1900 | They were wiped out by Venice in 1340 | They moved to Spain and became a kingdom there
test: A film shows only cheerful free sailors rowing a Venetian galley. What does it leave out? | Many rowers were prisoners, captives or enslaved | Galleys had no oars at all and moved only by sail | Only children rowed galleys | Galleys were rowed by horses walking in a wheel
test: If Venice had been built on open farmland instead of a lagoon, what would most likely be different? | It would have been much easier for armies to attack | It would have had more ships | It would have been much richer from the spice trade | Nothing, because a lagoon gives no protection to a city
test: why'd the Ottomans want Constantinople so bad, it's just one city | It controls the straits between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean | It had the only gold mine in Europe | It was the only city with a harbour | The sultan was born there and wanted his home town back from the Greeks
test: bloody hell, a thousand years? How long did the Republic of Venice last really | About a thousand years, until 1797 | About a hundred years, until 1500 | About fifty years, until 1453 | It still exists today as a republic
test: Your friend thinks the Mediterranean was the centre of world trade forever. What changed after 1500? | Ocean routes grew and it slowly became less central | It dried up and could no longer be sailed | Ships were banned from it by the Pope | Nothing ever changed there
test: A boy from a Balkan village is taken away and trained as a Janissary. Which system is this? | The devshirme | The encomienda | Serfdom | The Commonwealth
test: Which list fits the peoples of the Ottoman Empire? | Turks, Greeks, Arabs, Armenians, Slavs and Jews | Only Turks, with nobody else allowed to live there | Vikings, Saxons, Normans and Danes from the north | Aztecs, Incas and Maya from across the Atlantic Ocean
test: Mehmed II takes Constantinople in 1453. Which empire ends? | The Byzantine Empire | The Roman Republic | The Mughal Empire | The Republic of Genoa
test: Venice's Arsenal could finish ships quickly one after another. Why did a sea power need that? | To replace and grow its fleet fast in war and trade | To sell ships to the Ottomans only | To build houses on the lagoon | To keep its workers busy and out of trouble in the city
exam: Ascertain the geographical setting upon which the city of Venice was constructed. | A cluster of islands within a shallow lagoon of the upper Adriatic | A promontory upon the Black Sea adjacent to the Bosporus | An elevated plateau of central Anatolia | The banks of the River Rhine
exam: Identify the event of 1453 whereby the Byzantine Empire was terminated. | The capture of Constantinople by Sultan Mehmed II | The naval engagement at Lepanto | The dissolution of the Republic of Venice by Napoleon | The conquest of Crete by Venice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequences of the battle of Lepanto in 1571? | The Ottoman fleet was rebuilt within about a year, and Venice nonetheless lost Cyprus | Ottoman naval power was permanently extinguished, and Cyprus was restored to Venice | Venice thereby recovered Constantinople | The Ottoman Empire was thereupon dissolved
exam: State the year in which the Republic of Venice was terminated, and by whom. | In 1797, by Napoleon | In 1453, by Mehmed II | In 1571, by the Pope | In 1336, by the Doge
